{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Degor1HP41STRPL7LqAZat","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HU7s7m3dBSsuEu26xfH7mz","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments Review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. Review the paper below thoroughly and produce a structured review.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"When Does Structured Pruning Kill Reasoning? A Functional Analysis of Pruning Sensitivity in Reasoning LLMs\"\n\nThe paper conducts a systematic empirical study of structured pruning on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, comparing four pruning methods (Standard Taylor, magnitude-only, CKA-aware RASP, and learned-gate GRASPrune) across three sparsity levels (20%, 25%, 30%) with two recovery paradigms (SFT, SFT+GRPO). Key findings:\n\n1. At 20% structured sparsity, GSM8K accuracy collapses from 83.2% to 7.6–22.4% depending on method. Best recovery (GRASPrune) achieves only 47.7% of baseline — contradicting Self-Healing (2025)'s 95% recovery for unstructured pruning.\n2. CKA functional overlap (split-half ρ=0.92) does not predict per-structure recoverability (β=0.08, p=0.12). Pruning sensitivity is predominantly layer-level (ICC=0.73).\n3. GRPO adds only ~5pp over SFT alone, with no pruning×recovery interaction (p=0.54).\n4. Cross-benchmark: 76.8% MMLU retention vs 30% MATH-500 retention — structured pruning selectively destroys reasoning chains while preserving factual recall.\n\n## Detailed Paper Content\n\n**Methods (Section 3):**\n- 4 pruning strategies: Standard Taylor (|w·∇L|), RASP (compound score S(h)=I(h)×(1-F(h))^α with CKA overlap F(h) and per-layer 40% floor), Magnitude-only, Gradient-only, GRASPrune (learned gates + rescale compensation), Bonsai (gradient-free activation-based).\n- Recovery pipeline: Stage 1 SFT warmup (teacher-forcing on GSM8K train gold solutions, 3 epochs, lr 2e-5), Stage 2 GRPO (2000 steps, group size 8, KL coeff 0.04).\n- CKA overlap: F(h) = max_{h' in same layer} CKA(X_h, X_{h'}), linear kernel, 256 calibration samples.\n\n**Experimental Setup (Section 4):**\n- Model: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B (28 layers, 28 heads/layer, 784 total, 224 analyzed)\n- Sparsity: 20%, 25%, 30%\n- Primary eval: GSM8K (1319 test, full eval, 95% CIs). Secondary: MMLU (5-shot), HumanEval (0-shot), MATH-500 (4-shot).\n- McNemar's test for pairwise significance; mixed-effects regression for per-structure analysis.\n- Identical hyperparameters across all pruned models for recovery.\n\n**Results:**\n\nTable 1 (Post-prune 20%): Random=4.2%, Standard=7.6%±1.4, Magnitude=18.3%, Gradient=20.1%, Bonsai=19.5%, RASP=22.4%±2.3, GRASPrune=26.8%.\n\nTable 2 (Multi-seed RASP): Seed 42=22.4%, Seed 123=15.8%, Seed 7=28.1%. Mean±SD = 22.1±6.2.\n\nTable 3 (Recovery pipeline 20%): Standard: 7.6→22.1→26.3% (ratio 0.316). Bonsai: 19.5→31.2% (0.375). RASP: 22.4→28.5→33.8% (0.406). GRASPrune: 26.8→35.2→39.7% (0.477).\n\nTable 4 (Scoring ablation): RASP=22.4, Gradient-only=20.1, Magnitude=18.3, Standard=7.6, Random=4.2. CKA marginal = 2-4pp.\n\nTable 5 (Mixed-effects regression, N=224): CKA β=0.08, p=0.12 (ns). Weight norm β=0.19, p=0.003**. Gradient importance β=0.14, p=0.018*. Activation magnitude β=0.12, p=0.041*. Marginal R²=0.04, Conditional R²=0.31, ICC(layer)=0.73.\n\nTable 6 (CKA reliability): Split-half ρ=0.92, 256v512 corr=0.89, 512v1024 corr=0.95.\n\nTable 7 (2×2 ANOVA): RASP SFT=28.5→SFT+GRPO=33.8 (+5.3). Standard SFT=22.1→26.3 (+4.2). Interaction F=0.38, p=0.54 (ns). Recovery main effect p=0.008, Pruning main effect p=0.003.\n\nTable 8 (Sparsity sensitivity): RASP 20%→22.4→33.8 (0.406), 25%→10.2→20.3 (0.244), 30%→2.8→collapse. Standard 20%→7.6→26.3 (0.316), 25%→2.1→14.7 (0.177), 30%→0.5→collapse.\n\nTable 9 (Cross-benchmark RASP recovered 20%): MMLU 54.8→42.1 (76.8%), GSM8K 83.2→33.8 (40.6%), HumanEval 41.5→18.3 (44.1%), MATH-500 52.3→15.7 (30.0%).\n\nAppendix B: GRPO dynamics — reward 0.31 (RASP) vs 0.24 (Standard), clipped ratio 0.18 vs 0.22.\nAppendix C: SFT loss 1.42 (RASP) vs 1.68 (Standard), EOS rate 0.94 vs 0.87.\n\n## Review Context — Imagined Experiments\n\nThe full experimental design includes these experiments. Check coverage:\n1. Full GSM8K Eval (plan_001) — covered in Table 1\n2. SFT Warmup Recovery (plan_002) — covered in Table 3\n3. GRPO Recovery (plan_003) — covered in Table 3\n4. Full GSM8K Eval for recovered models (plan_004) — covered in Table 3\n5. GRASPrune baseline (plan_005) — covered in Tables 1, 3\n6. Multi-seed RASP (plan_006) — covered in Table 2\n7. Scoring ablation (plan_007) — covered in Table 4\n8. CKA-Recoverability correlation (plan_008) — covered in Table 5\n9. SFT-only recovery comparison / interaction (plan_009) — covered in Table 7\n10. 25% sparsity (plan_010) — covered in Table 8\n11. 30% sparsity probe (plan_011) — covered in Table 8\n12. Extended evaluation (plan_012) — covered in Table 9\n13. Bonsai baseline (plan_013) — covered in Tables 1, 3\n14. CKA reliability (plan_014) — covered in Table 6\n\n## Your Task\n\nProduce a structured review with these sections:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focus on methodology and experimental design)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on methodology and experimental design)\n4. **Questions to Authors** (specific, numbered)\n5. **Missing Experiments or Controls** (what would strengthen the paper)\n6. **Minor Issues** (presentation, clarity)\n7. **Overall Assessment** (1-2 paragraphs)\n8. **Score** (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n9. **Confidence** (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Focus on methodological soundness, statistical rigor, experimental completeness, and whether conclusions are supported by evidence."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":35507,"cache_read_input_tokens":22962,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":35507},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"abb965cf-b673-4ef3-ad15-a808ac75ed71","uuid":"7fb792a8-81af-4052-8edb-608cbb1c2ed8"}
